'''2.''' Consisting of two separate, closely related, and equal members; twofold; double; specifically, consisting of or forming twins or a pair: as, ''twin'' children.
{{smaller block|<poem>An apple cleft in two is not more ''twin''
Than these two creatures. ''Shak.'', T. N., v. 1. 302.</poem>
Parrots with ''twin'' cherries in their beak.
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, i. 38.}}}}
'''3.''' One, each, or either of two; one of a pair, specifically of two born at a birth: as, a ''twin'' brother or sister.
{{smaller block|<poem>The water up-stod, thurgh godes migt,
On ''twinne'' half, also a wal up-rigt.</poem>
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3248.}}
A Boat ''twin''-sister of the crescent-moon!
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Peter Bell.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''bot.'', growing in pairs; didymous.—'''5.''' Consisting of two chief parts alike, or nearly alike, and held firmly together: as, a ''twin'' bottle; a ''twin'' vase. The plural is used in the same sense: as, ''twin'' vases.—'''6.''' In ''entom.'', geminate: applied to spots, punctures, spines, etc., which are close together in pairs, and distant from others.—{{smaller|'''The Twin Brothers''' or '''Brethren,''' Castor and Pollux; the Twins.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>These be the Great ''Twin Brethren''
To whom the Dorians pray.</poem>
{{right|''Macaulay'', Battle of Lake Regillus, st. 40.}}}}

