the twinned octahedron of fig. 2 results, the twinning-plane being here a face of the octahedron; such twins are common with spinel, and are hence called ''spinel twins.'' This is also called a ''juxtaposition-'' or ''contact-twin'', in distinction from a ''penetration-twin'', such as is represented in fig. 3, where each crystal is complete and interpenetrates the other. If the molecular reversal is often repeated in the growth of a crystal, a ''polysynthetic twin'' may result, consisting of successive thin layers or lamellæ of two sets, alternately in reversed position to each other. This is common among the plagioclase feldspars, and is the cause of the fine striation often observed on a cleavage-surface. (See ''albite twin'' and ''pericline twin'', below.)
